WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.800
Windows volume leveling

00:00:01.812 --> 00:00:02.908
rebuilt for Linux

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.368
audio

00:00:03.894 --> 00:00:05.851
Windows Loudness Equalization

00:00:05.993 --> 00:00:06.948
the feature that

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:08.021
evens out playback

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:08.481
volume

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:10.049
has been reverse-engineered

00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:10.686
from the Windows

00:00:10.498 --> 00:00:11.818
audio driver and

00:00:11.642 --> 00:00:12.962
ported to PipeWire

00:00:12.774 --> 00:00:13.622
on Linux

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:14.959
It evens out

00:00:14.771 --> 00:00:15.643
the volume of

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:16.622
each application's

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:17.412
playback stream

00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:18.780
so quiet and

00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:19.641
loud parts play

00:00:19.476 --> 00:00:20.278
at a similar

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:20.679
level

00:00:21.206 --> 00:00:21.960
Under the hood

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:22.880
it sums power

00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:24.130
into twenty-five frequency

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:25.109
bands with edges

00:00:24.921 --> 00:00:25.958
from one hundred

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.102
hertz to twenty-four

00:00:26.914 --> 00:00:27.809
thousand hertz

00:00:27.951 --> 00:00:28.623
and the gain

00:00:28.435 --> 00:00:29.366
it applies is

00:00:29.178 --> 00:00:30.215
capped at twenty

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:30.887
decibels

00:00:31.422 --> 00:00:32.436
Full story on

00:00:32.247 --> 00:00:33.143
the dashboard
